{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c533","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a06fb9-e0fc-7072-adfc-69f8598926a4"},"sequence":244,"recorded_at":1780531400000483,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"694249df-ed90-4909-b924-7901358b9328","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"694249df-ed90-4909-b924-7901358b9328","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"694249df-ed90-4909-b924-7901358b9328"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"2d294711-f329-4626-9473-600d6ce38d35"},"task_id":"2d294711-f329-4626-9473-600d6ce38d35","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"2d294711-f329-4626-9473-600d6ce38d35","chunk":"Read text file `/tmp/r4/verdict.md`.\n1|# TASK-260830-32jeti round 4 — reviewer verdict: CHANGES REQUESTED\n2|\n3|CR `CR-TASK-260830-32jeti-1` rev 1, base `57afcc6`, candidate tree\n4|`f886873ee208e9176973c70735bfd40be69adf23`.\n5|\n6|Working tree verified byte-identical to the candidate tree before and after\n7|every mutant (temp-index `git write-tree` = `f886873…` both times; every\n8|mutated file restored from a `cp` backup and sha256-verified, never\n9|`git checkout`).\n10|\n11|## Baseline (re-run by me, not accepted from evidence)\n12|\n13|| Check | Result |\n14|| --- | --- |\n15|| `go build ./...` | exit 0 |\n16|| `go vet ./...` | exit 0 |\n17|| `GOOS=windows go vet ./...` | exit 0 |\n18|| `gofmt -l ./internal` | empty |\n19|| `go test ./... -count=1` | 15 packages ok |\n20|| `go test ./internal/provhost/ -race` | ok 11.751s |\n21|| coverage | provhost 86.4%, provider 97.0% (matches the LOGBOOK claim) |\n22|\n23|## G-A — the derived arm census\n24|\n25|**Ratio: 162 derived arms / 162 witnessed, both directions green, across 14\n26|production files.** The denominator is not the witness list: it is rebuilt by\n27|`refusalArmsIn` walking every non-test `.go` file in the package with\n28|`os.ReadDir` + `go/parser.ParseFile`. I attacked the denominator itself\n29|rather than reading it.\n30|\n31|- **Does the walk visit `runner_windows.go`?** Yes. `os.ReadDir` +\n32|  `go/parser.ParseFile` ignore build constraints entirely — there is no\n33|  `go/build` context in the path, so `//go:build windows` is invisible to the\n34|  scan. Proven, not read: mutant **C3** planted\n35|  `var _ = &frameFault{detail: \"windows planted arm\", member: \"planted\"}`\n36|  into `runner_windows.go` and `TestDerivedRefusalArmsAreAllWitnessed` went\n37|  **red** on the unwitnessed arm, while the darwin build stayed green. The\n38|  control **C4** (same plant in `runner_unix.go`) is red the same way.\n39|  This is structurally *unlike* leaf-1 F20: that was CI/compile blindness to\n40|  `os_windows.go`, not walk blindness. The two failure modes are also\n41|  distinguishable here — a file the walk **cannot parse** fails the derivation\n42|  outright (`parser.ParseFile` error → `t.Fatalf`), a file it **does not\n43|  visit** would drop arms and trip the reverse witness check. Neither is live.\n44|- **Do `idempotency.go`, `identity.go`, `spawn.go` contribute witnessed arms?**\n45|  Yes. Mutant **C2** (scan skips `identity.go`) reddens *both*\n46|  `TestDerivedRefusalArmsAreAllWitnessed` and `TestWitnessedArmsAreAllDerived`.\n47|  Mutant **C5** (one witness arm string corrupted) reddens both directions.\n48|  Mutant **C8** (new inline arm in `probe.go`) reddens the forward check.\n49|- **Was the floor raised, and can it drift?** Raised 60 → 162, which equals the\n50|  exact derived count. It can still drift down invisibly: mutant **C1**\n51|  (`refusalArmCensusFloor = 1`) **survives** the whole suite. Round 3's\n52|  conclusion therefore still holds at the larger surface: the floor is a\n53|  redundant tripwire, the witness set is what is load-bearing (C2/C5 red).\n54|- **The round-3 wrapper shape.** Mutant **C6** replaces an inline refusal with\n55|  a `refuseWarningOrder()` wrapper: **red on both directions** (derived count\n56|  drops to 161, below the floor, and the witness orphans). The residual blind\n57|  shape the LOGBOOK states — a *newly added* arm reachable only through a\n58|  wrapper, so nothing orphans and the floor never drops — is real (**C9b**\n59|  survives) and is stated honestly in both the file header and the LOGBOOK.\n60|\n61|## G-B — the mutant battery\n62|\n63|**106 narrowing mutants applied, 76 killed, 30 survived.** Two survivors\n64|(C1, C9b) are the stated bounds confirmed above. **28 are genuine coverage\n65|gaps.** 12 further mutants were discarded as invalid (Go rejected them for\n66|unused variables / undefined names — a compile-error \"kill\" proves nothing,\n67|so each was re-expressed in a compiling form and re-run).\n68|\n69|Prior leaves: `internal/provider` is **untouched** by this delta, so leaf 1's\n70|83-mutant battery cannot resurrect from it; the package is green at 97.0%.\n71|Leaf 2's provhost surface is touched only by `doc.go` (comment-only — the file\n72|holds exactly one non-comment line, `package provhost`) and by\n73|`refusal_arm_inventory_test.go`, whose derivation code is **byte-identical to\n74|HEAD** (only the header comment, the floor constant, and three witness-append\n75|expressions changed). The header change is a *correction*: the old text\n76|claimed non-literal constructor arguments become `expr:` obligations, which\n77|the code never did. All 60 leaf-2 arms remain witnessed and still refuse at\n78|the production entry.\n79|\n80|### F1 (blocking) — closed vocabularies are not proven closed\n81|\n82|Eight closed enums accept a silently added member with the whole suite green.\n83|Each is a permissive-direction hole: the harness would pass a provider\n84|advertising a value the contract never defined.\n85|\n86|| Mutant | Widened enum | Effect |\n87|| --- | --- | --- |\n88|| P14 | `probeCapabilityStatuses` + `\"partial\"` | SURVIVED |\n89|| P15 | `probeCapabilityEvidence` + `\"assumed\"` | SURVIVED |\n90|| P16 | `probeArchitectures` + `\"386\"` | SURVIVED |\n91|| P17 | `probePlatforms` + `\"freebsd\"` | SURVIVED |\n92|| Q16 | `quiesceBlockers` + `\"other\"` | SURVIVED |\n93|| ID1 | `identityKinds` + `\"legacy_alias\"` | SURVIVED |\n94|| ID2 | `identifyConfidences` + `\"guess\"` | SURVIVED |\n95|| ID3 | `identifyEvidence` + `\"guessed\"` | SURVIVED |\n96|\n97|This is not a missing mechanism — it is a mechanism applied to three surfaces\n98|and not the other eight. `TestManifestRegistriesAreDerivedFromSpec` re-reads\n99|the pinned `SPEC.md` through `specdoc.Load()` and derives operations,\n100|capability names and platforms from the Section 7.3 example; **MA17**\n101|(widening `manifestPlatforms`) is duly **killed** by it.\n102|`TestMutationOperationsAreDerivedFromSpec` (K1b, K2 killed) and\n103|`TestProfileMappingIsPinnedToSection77` (PR1, PR3 killed) do the same for the\n104|keyed-operation set and the Section 7.7 table. The eight enums above got a\n105|transcription and a hand-written sample instead.\n106|\n107|Every one of them is stated verbatim in the pinned document and is derivable\n108|the same way — e.g. `SafeBoundaryProof.blockers` at `SPEC.md:2907`,\n109|`identity_kind` at `:2086`, capability evidence at `:2873`, identify\n110|confidence and `matched_evidence` at `:3075`.\n111|\n112|The LOGBOOK's \"COMPLEMENTS, NOT SAMPLES: … 4 statuses × 2 enabled swept\" is\n113|true as far as it goes and does not reach this: sweeping the four known\n114|statuses proves the rule over those four, not that the vocabulary contains\n115|exactly those four. A conformance harness wrong in the permissive direction\n116|passes a non-conforming provider — the named failure mode for this leaf.\n117|\n118|(Checked separately: the implemented `quiesceBlockers` set is *correct*. The\n119|`background_active|input_not_blocked|process_open` vocabulary at `SPEC.md:4183`\n120|and `:7223` belongs to `BridgeSafeBoundary`/RPC `SafeBoundary`, different\n121|objects. No fidelity defect — only an unproven one.)\n122|\n123|### F2 (blocking) — positional blind spots in ordered / registry loops\n124|\n125|Round 3 closed index 0 for the two manifest *order* loops (MA2, MA3 killed by\n126|`operations_first_substituted` / `capability_first_substituted`). The same\n127|shape is still live at four sibling positions:\n128|\n129|| Mutant | Loop narrowed | Effect |\n130|| --- | --- | --- |\n131|| MA1r | `checkManifestCapabilities` order loop skips the **last** name | SURVIVED |\n132|| MA4r | `checkManifestOperations` order loop skips the **last** operation | SURVIVED |\n133|| MA14r | `checkManifestPlatforms` vocabulary loop skips the **first** element | SURVIVED |\n134|| P3r / P3s | `checkProbeCapabilities` missing-key loop skips **first** / **last** registry key | SURVIVED |\n135|\n136|MA1r/MA4r/MA14r are permissive: a manifest whose last operation or last\n137|capability name is wrong, or whose first platform is `\"aix\"`, is accepted under\n138|the mutant and nothing reddens. P3r/P3s are attribution gaps — a probe missing\n139|`native_resume` still refuses, but through a different arm, so the\n140|\"miss a registry key\" obligation is proven only for `prompt_spawn`.\n141|\n142|### F3 (blocking) — NUL injection into argv and env literals has no negative test\n143|\n144|| Mutant | Gate removed | Effect |\n145|| --- | --- | --- |\n146|| SP4 | `strings.ContainsRune(value, 0)` in `checkSpawnArgv` | SURVIVED |\n147|| SP5 | `strings.ContainsRune(text, 0)` in `checkSpawnEnvLiterals` | SURVIVED |\n148|\n149|These are the two SpawnPlan members the trusted terminal backend feeds to\n150|`exec`. The length and grammar rules around them are tested (SP7, SP11, SP15,\n151|SP16, SP18 all killed); the NUL rule is the only clause on those members with\n152|no fixture at all — a delete-only mutant of it is invisible.\n153|\n154|### F4 (blocking) — identity provider correlation: same-length wrong provider unproven\n155|\n156|**ID14** narrows `if provider != wantProviderID` to\n157|`provider != wantProviderID && len(provider) != len(wantProviderID)` and\n158|**SURVIVES**. `TestCheckIdentityNamesAnotherProvider` drives the Antigravity\n159|example against `\"codex\"` — 11 characters against 5. `\"claude\"` against\n160|`\"gemini\"` are both 6, and nothing distinguishes them. This is the\n161|`invalid_config` gate that stops the host correlating one provider's identity\n162|record to another provider's session; its only negative fixture is\n163|length-separable.\n164|\n165|### F5 — upper bounds with no boundary fixture (10)\n166|\n167|Each is `bound → bound+1`, every one SURVIVED:\n168|\n169|| Mutant | Bound | File |\n170|| --- | --- | --- |\n171|| Q14 | quiesce `provider_version` ≤128 | quiesce.go |\n172|| P10 | probe `provider_version` ≤128 | probe.go |\n173|| MA7 | provider-id grammar `{0,31}` | manifest.go |\n174|| ID9 | opaque key grammar `{0,63}` | identity.go |\n175|| ID10 | opaque key first char `[a-z]` → `[a-z0-9]` | identity.go |\n176|| ID15 | identity `provider_version_range` ≤256 | identity.go |\n177|| ID16 | identity `native_session_id` ≤512 | identity.go |\n178|| SP6 | argv element count ≤128 | spawn.go |\n179|| SP8 | argv total ≤65536 bytes | spawn.go |\n180|| SP12 | env-name length ≤128 | spawn.go |\n181|\n182|The pattern is inconsistent rather than absent: identity's `provider_version`\n183|*has* a 129 fixture (ID17 killed) and manifest's `display_name` has one\n184|(MA9 killed), while the same member in probe and quiesce has only the empty\n185|lower-bound row. SP8 has no fixture on either side — the 65,536-byte argv\n186|total is entirely unexercised.\n187|\n188|### F6 — claims in comments and tests that nothing measures\n189|\n190|- **K5** — `MutationOperations` says \"The result is a copy; the set cannot be\n191|  mutated through it.\" Returning `keyedOperations` directly instead of the\n192|  `append([]Operation(nil), …)` copy **survives**: no test mutates the returned\n193|  slice and re-reads. (`Capabilities()` carries the identical comment and the\n194|  identical absence of a test.)\n195|- **Q4b** — `DecodeQuiesceProof`'s safe-rule comment says \"each conjunct below\n196|  carries a fixture with only it violated\". Neutralising `backgroundNull`\n197|  **survives**, because `rawNullableBool` returns `false` for null, so\n198|  `backgroundNull || !background` reduces to `!background` and no fixture can\n199|  isolate that conjunct. The other eight conjuncts are individually killed\n200|  (Q1b, Q2b, Q3b, Q5b, Q6b, Q7b, Q8, Q9) — the claim is true for 8 of 9 and the\n201|  ninth is unreachable, not untested. Either drop the redundant disjunct or\n202|  restate the comment.\n203|- **`TestCapabilityGatePrecedesCall`** — the negative subtests construct\n204|  `runner := &scriptRunner{}` and assert `runner.spawned() != 0`, but the runner\n205|  is never handed to anything: `RequireCapability` takes no `Runner`. The\n206|  assertion cannot fail. The 21-tuple sweep itself is good (derived from\n207|  `Capabilities()` × 3 non-available statuses, count-asserted, with a real\n208|  positive control that does spawn exactly one process), but the LOGBOOK's\n209|  \"with zero processes started\" half is not measured by it — it follows\n210|  structurally from the signature, which is a different and weaker claim.\n211|- Minor, non-blocking: `checkSpawnArgv` and `checkSpawnEnvNames` report\n212|  `\"spawn argv is empty or longer than 128\"` / `\"spawn env_names exceed 64\n213|  entries\"` when the member is not an array at all. The refusal is correct, the\n214|  detail names the wrong rule.\n215|\n216|### What held up under attack (76 kills, selected)\n217|\n218|The safe-lie rule (8 of 9 conjuncts individually killed), the\n219|`enabled ⇒ available` probe rule and the pure `CapabilityUsable` gate (P1, P2),\n220|the per-key capability value sweep at both ends (P4, P5), sorted-unique across\n221|every array member including the first pair (P12, P13), the profile table and\n222|both its refusal seats (PR1–PR5), the SpawnPlan mapping equality in both\n223|directions including the standard-omission lie (SP1, SP2), env-literal\n224|disjointness (SP3), destination-native cwd (SP13, SP14), the whole idempotency\n225|key surface (K1b, K2, K3, K4), identity's subject/session equality, the\n226|Antigravity realm precondition, the Windows-drive and UNC opaque-path prefixes,\n227|and the manifest schema/semver/registry-count rules. `TestEveryArmWitness\n228|RefusesAtTheProductionEntry` co-killed a large share of these, which is the\n229|inventory doing exactly what it was built for.\n230|\n231|## G-C — bounds and the logbook\n232|\n233|The `LOGBOOK.md` entry is the mechanism, not a leaf summary: it records the AST\n234|derivation and both directions, the census-floor reasoning, the blind shapes as\n235|stated-not-fixed, the `DecodeQuiesceProof` defect the new fixtures caught\n236|before review (safe:true with `open_child_count:1`), and the restated bounds.\n237|That closes the round-3 item.\n238|\n239|Standing bounds re-verified by me, all unchanged:\n240|\n241|- `provhost` does not import `internal/provider` — `go list -deps` returns 0\n242|  matches. `spawn.go` and `identity.go` do not change this; they import only\n243|  `internal/scalar` (already a dependency), `encoding/json`, `regexp`,\n244|  `strings`.\n245|- `provhost` still has no importers — grep across all `.go` outside the package\n246|  is empty.\n247|- No top-level `cmd/`.\n248|- `U2` (process-group kill on macOS) — nothing in the new files touches process\n249|  teardown; `runner_windows.go` and `runner_unix.go` are unchanged from HEAD and\n250|  no new test asserts detached-descendant knowledge.\n251|- `S1`'s ExecRunner fork/exec red reappeared as an unrelated one-off during this\n252|  battery (3 of ~110 full-package runs, in `TestExecRunnerWritesOneTerminatedFrame`,\n253|  `TestExecRunnerEchoRoundTrip`, `TestExecRunnerReportsCrashExit`). It stays an\n254|  observed environmental flake; I re-ran every affected mutant under a scoped\n255|  `-run` mask so no verdict here rests on it.\n256|- README does not overclaim: it says the *manifest* registries are derived from\n257|  the pinned example and makes no derivation claim for the probe, quiescence or\n258|  identity vocabularies. F1 is a test gap, not a README honesty failure.\n259|\n260|## Verdict\n261|\n262|**Changes requested.** F1 is the deciding finding: this leaf is the conformance\n263|harness, and eight of its eleven closed vocabularies would pass a provider\n264|advertising a value the contract never defined, with the suite green. F2, F3\n265|and F4 are the same failure in smaller pieces — a complement asserted over a\n266|hand-picked sample. The spec-derivation mechanism to fix F1 already exists in\n267|this file set and is applied three times; extending it is mechanical.\n268|\n269|Production code looks correct throughout — every mutant here narrows a rule the\n270|implementation gets right. The gap is entirely in what the tests prove.\n271|\n272|Suggested order: F1 (derive the eight vocabularies from `specdoc` the way\n273|`TestManifestRegistriesAreDerivedFromSpec` does), F3 and F4 (two and one\n274|fixtures), F2 (last/first-element substitution rows at the four loops), F5 (ten\n275|`bound+1` rows), F6 (one aliasing test, two comment corrections, one vacuous\n276|assertion removed or wired).\n277|\n278|Harness, mutant specs and raw results:\n279|`.temp/TASK-260830-32jeti/{mutate.py,b1.json,b1b.json,b2.json,b2r.json,b3.json,b4.json,b5.json,b6.json,b7.json}`"}}}
